Agent Response: Yes — I can help you get organized and prepare specific, useful questions for your attorney and for anticipating what the judge or opposing counsel may ask at the status conference. Below is a focused checklist and sets of questions you can use with Sarah Chen and to rehearse answers for the conference.

If you’d like, I can also draft a short email to Sarah with these questions or create a one‑page prep sheet you can bring to the conference — tell me which and I’ll draft it.

1) Quick clarifying questions for you (before I tailor further)
- Is Sarah Chen your attorney for the eviction case, the custody case, or both?  
- Do you have a separate family-law attorney handling custody?  
- Is the status conference only for the eviction matter (county civil/eviction hearing) or is the custody matter being discussed in the same docket?  
Answering these will let me tailor the questions more precisely.

2) High‑priority questions to ask your attorney (Sarah Chen)
Label these as “need to know before the status conference”
- What is the purpose of this status conference in this court (scheduling only, settlement exploration, case management, or readiness hearing)? What should I expect?  
- What is the judge likely to ask me or opposing counsel at the conference? How should I respond concisely?  
- What relief are we asking the court for (dismissal, additional time, trial date, emergency relief)? What does success look like at this conference?  
- Are there any immediate deadlines (discovery cutoff, motions, disclosures) we must meet after the conference?  
- What documents or evidence should I bring and have organized for the conference or for any follow-up? (Ask for a prioritized list.)  
- Are there local Denver County rules or procedures (forms, page limits, filings) I should know about? Where can I find them?  
- Do we need to exchange any documents or witness lists with opposing counsel before the next deadline? If so, what and by when?  
- Should my family-law/custody counsel be looped in (if different) because of the other parent/custody overlap? Could housing or eviction proceedings affect custody issues here?  
- Are there settlement or mediation options we should prepare for now? Do you recommend opening settlement discussions before/after the status conference?  
- What questions should I be prepared to answer under oath (if any) and how should I keep my answers concise?  
- If the judge asks for a timeline or factual summary, can you help me prepare a one‑page timeline and one‑paragraph statement of issues?  
- What witnesses (if any) do we want to call later, and should I get brief written statements now?  
- Are there likely to be any emergency motions (e.g., temporary relief) we should consider?  
- What are the worst-case and best-case next steps after the status conference (timing to trial, possible orders, costs)?

3) Anticipated questions the judge might ask (and how to prepare to answer)
- “What is the current status of settlement discussions?” — prepare a short sentence: whether settlement been attempted and current position (open/decline).  
- “What relief are you seeking and why?” — have a 1–2 sentence summary of your requested outcome and main legal/factual basis.  
- “Are there any disputes over evidence or discovery?” — know whether any documents remain outstanding and give timelines.  
- “How much time do the parties need to prepare or complete discovery?” — estimate realistically; ask your attorney beforehand what time is reasonable.  
- “Do you anticipate any complex evidentiary issues or need for expert testimony?” — ask attorney if any experts are anticipated and why.  
- “Is there any urgent issue the court should know about?” — if housing stability, safety, or immediate financial hardship exists, be prepared to state it briefly and ask your attorney if an emergency motion is appropriate.

4) Anticipated questions opposing counsel may ask (and prep)
- Questions aimed at timeline and facts: “When did nonpayment/notice occur?”, “What communications happened and when?” — have dates and copies of messages/letters ready.  
- Questions about finances: “Can you pay arrears?”, “What proof of payment do you have?” — bring bank records, rent ledger, receipts.  
- If custody overlap is relevant: “How will any eviction impact the children’s living arrangements?” — prepare factual, non‑argumentative statements about current living arrangements and practical impacts. Coordinate with family-law counsel for strategy.  
- Questions to impeach credibility: opposing counsel may ask about inconsistencies — review your timeline and be consistent in answers. Don’t guess; say “I don’t recall, I will verify” when appropriate.

5) Documents and exhibits to prepare and bring (organized, labeled)
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages).  
- Notices sent/received (3‑day notice, cure or quit, eviction notice) with dates.  
- Rent ledger showing payments, arrears, dates. Bank statements or receipts showing rent payments.  
- Any written communications (texts, emails, letters) between you and the landlord or opposing party; print and annotate with dates.  
- Court filings and pleadings already filed in this eviction case and in the custody case (if relevant).  
- Any temporary custody orders, child support orders, or family-court documents that may relate to housing/custody.  
- Photo evidence (conditions), repair requests, habitability issues, if relevant.  
- Witness contact list and short statements (one paragraph each) of what each witness will testify to.  
- A one‑page chronological timeline of critical events (dates and 1‑line description) — the judge will appreciate brevity.

6) How to present answers in court (practical tips)
- Be concise. Practice 1–2 sentence answers to likely judge questions.  
- Stick to facts you can document. If you don’t remember details, say so and offer to provide supporting documents.  
- Do not volunteer unnecessary narrative. Let your attorney speak for legal argumentation. You can provide factual clarifications when asked.  
- If asked about custody matters, avoid arguing legal points — state facts and ask your counsel to address legal relevance.

7) Strategy topics to discuss with your attorney now
- Whether to seek temporary relief or emergency orders and standards for that.  
- Whether to exchange discovery or propound specific requests now.  
- Witness strategy — who is essential vs. nice-to-have.  
- Coordinating between eviction and custody counsel (if different) to avoid conflicting filings or harmful disclosures.  
- Cost estimate and timeline to trial; what steps shorten or lengthen that timeline.  
- Any options for mediation and what minimum terms you’d accept.

8) Practical prep checklist (what to complete before the status conference)
- Create and print the 1‑page timeline and a one‑paragraph “statement of the case” for the judge.  
- Assemble the documents list above in a folder (physical or digital), labeled by topic.  
- Make a short list of witnesses and their contact info; get short written statements if possible.  
- Meet with Sarah to review likely questions and rehearse 2–3 concise answers you’ll give.  
- Confirm whether you need to file anything with the court prior to the conference (status report, proposed scheduling order).  
- Ask your attorney if you need to be present in person or whether the conference is telephonic.

9) Sample concise statements you can practice (tailor to facts)
- “We’ve attempted settlement; the landlord has rejected our most recent proposal. We are asking the court for a scheduling order so both sides can complete discovery.”  
- “My priority is preserving stable housing for the children while these issues are resolved. I can provide proof of payments and communications I relied upon.”  
Practice these with Sarah so she can correct wording.

10) Offer I can do next
- Draft an email to Sarah Chen with the prioritized questions above (you can send it as-is or edit).  
- Create that one‑page timeline and 1‑paragraph case summary from the facts you provide.  
- Make a numbered checklist you can bring to the status conference.

Tell me which of those you want me to draft (email to Sarah, one‑page timeline, or both), and answer the two quick clarifying questions at the top if you want me to tailor the materials further.
