Assistant: Good — below is a focused, practical checklist of the questions to ask your attorney and the specific things to prepare so you’ll be ready for the status conference and for likely questions from the judge or opposing counsel. I’ve grouped items so you can go through them with Sarah Chen efficiently.

Clarifying question before we go further
- Do you have separate cases (one eviction and one custody) or is this a single matter where both eviction and custody issues overlap? Also, what is the date of the status conference? (If you want, I can tailor the checklist to the exact date and timelines.)

1) High‑priority questions to ask your attorney (Sarah Chen)
- What will the judge expect at the status conference in Denver County (format, time, and what information should I be ready to state)?
- What specific questions do you expect the judge will ask me?
- What lines of questioning or specific claims do you expect opposing counsel will raise?
- What is our overall case strategy between now and the conference (goals, risks, and best/worst outcomes)?
- Are there any immediate deadlines (motions, disclosures, discovery) I must meet before the conference?
- Do we need any temporary orders or emergency relief before the status conference?
- Should we ask the judge for a continuance, settlement conference, or referral to mediation/parenting coordination?
- What documents or exhibits should I bring to court and which should you file in advance?
- Who will speak for me at the conference — you or me — and how should I be prepared if the judge asks me questions directly?
- What testimony or admissions should I absolutely avoid (phrasing that could harm our case)?
- How will custody issues (if separate) affect the eviction/possession issues, or vice versa?
- What are realistic settlement options and what is my negotiation bottom line?
- Are there local Denver County procedural rules or a particular judge’s preferences I should know about?
- Do you recommend I/you contact any witnesses (landlord, other parent, neighbors) before the conference?

2) Likely questions the judge may ask (eviction and civil status conference)
- For eviction: Who currently lives in the unit? Are there unpaid rent amounts or lease violations? What notice(s) were served and when? What remedy are you seeking (possession, rent, damages)?
- For custody overlap: Who has primary physical custody now? What temporary arrangements are in place? Is there any safety or CPS history?
- For case management: What issues remain unresolved? What discovery needs to occur? Proposed scheduling (deadlines for discovery, motions, trial date)?
- For settlement: Have the parties attempted to resolve this? Are you willing to mediate?

3) Likely questions from opposing counsel
- Questions about payment history, why rent wasn’t paid, repair requests, or the facts behind lease violations.
- For custody: routines, child schedules, income/support, childcare logistics, any allegations of neglect or abuse.
- Requests for admissions or factual concessions designed to narrow issues — ask Sarah which admissions are safe vs. harmful.

4) Documents to gather and bring (give copies to your lawyer and bring originals to court)
- Lease/rental agreement and any amendments; rent ledger and receipts (bank statements, money orders).
- Notices sent/received (pay or quit notices, eviction notices, repair requests, certified mail receipts).
- All written communications with the landlord/co-tenant/opposing parent (texts, emails, DM screenshots).
- Court filings, summons, complaint, answers, prior orders.
- Proof of service documents and timelines of events.
- Photographs or videos of the property condition, repairs, damages.
- Police reports, repair invoices, contractor estimates, receipts for repairs or expenses.
- For custody: school records, medical records, daycare/provider statements, CPS or social services reports (if any).
- Financial documents: recent pay stubs, bank statements, proof of benefits, tax returns (if income/support is contested).
- Witness contact info and short statements (neighbor, co‑tenant, landlord, daycare).

5) Financial & custody details to review with your attorney
- Exact arrears amount, calculation method, and payment history.
- Any partial payments and documentation of those payments.
- Your monthly income, expenses, and ability to cure arrears or propose a payment plan.
- Any pending or previous child support orders, recent compliance history.
- Parenting schedule currently in place and any incidents relevant to custody or safety.
- Whether any third parties (social services, schools) have records relevant to custody.

6) Preparation and practice items to request
- Ask Sarah for a short script of what to say if the judge asks you direct factual questions (one‑sentence answers for common questions).
- Role‑play with your attorney a brief direct Q&A so your answers are concise and legally safe.
- Ask her to mark which documents she will file and which you should bring.
- Confirm whether you should file any Exhibit List or Witness List before the conference.

7) Procedural & timeline questions
- What are the local deadlines for discovery, motions, settlement conference, and trial setting in Denver County civil division?
- How long does the judge typically give for resolving eviction arrears or scheduling a trial for possession?
- What motion practice do we anticipate (motion to dismiss, summary judgment, emergency relief)?

8) Strategy & settlement considerations to discuss
- Pros and cons of immediate settlement vs. continuing toward trial.
- Whether a payment plan or conditional dismissal is realistic and what terms protect you.
- Whether to request a parenting evaluation, temporary custody order, or supervised visitation (if custody is contested).
- Cost/fee exposure and whether attorney’s fees or costs might be recoverable by either side.

9) Courtroom demeanor and practical tips
- Be concise. Answer questions directly — don’t volunteer extra details.
- Refer legal/technical questions to your attorney (e.g., “I’ll let my attorney address that”).
- Bring 3 copies of any exhibits (judge, opposing counsel, your counsel).
- Dress neatly, be punctual, and turn phones off.
- If you don’t know an answer, say so — don’t guess.

10) Quick pre-conference checklist (what to do in the next 2 months)
- Gather and organize documents above; create a single folder (digital and paper) with labeled tabs.
- Send all relevant documents to Sarah and ask for a prioritized item list 3 weeks before the conference.
- Confirm with Sarah whether she will appear and whether you must appear.
- Schedule a short prep call or meeting with Sarah 1–2 weeks before the status conference to rehearse answers.
- Identify and contact key witnesses now; get short written statements if possible.
- Confirm filing deadlines and ensure necessary motions or disclosures are filed on time.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short email to Sarah Chen listing these questions and attaching a document checklist you can sign off on before the conference.
- Create a one‑page “what to say” script for the judge’s typical questions.
- Tailor all of the above if you confirm whether eviction and custody are separate matters and tell me the exact status conference date.

Tell me which of those follow-ups you want me to draft, or confirm the court date and whether these are separate cases and I’ll prepare the next item (email or script).

