  - Are there any immediate safety concerns for the child(ren)? Any domestic violence or protective orders in place or needed?
  - Are temporary custody or visitation arrangements required pending full hearing?
- Case narrowing / settlement
  - Have the parties attempted mediation or settlement discussions?
  - Are there any issues that can be resolved before trial?
- Enforcement / temporary relief (if eviction)
  - What temporary housing or relief is needed? Any requests for interim injunctions or stays?

C. Questions opposing counsel is likely to ask you (and what they may try to probe)
- Facts & timeline
  - When did the alleged breach/eviction events occur? Dates of missed rent, notices served, communications about repairs, etc.
- Financials
  - Current income, employment, benefits, bank balances, whether you paid rent and when.
- Parenting & stability
  - Daily routine with the children, childcare, schooling, who provides transportation, who lives in the household.
- Credibility & documents
  - Requests to produce records, communications (texts, emails), witness contact information.
- Motive/intent
  - Questions designed to show inconsistency or to paint your actions as neglectful or intentional noncompliance.

D. Documents and evidence checklist (gather now)
- Lease, rental agreement, any amendment, and landlord notices (eviction notice, cure notices)
- Rent payment records: bank transfers, cancelled checks, receipts, money order stubs
- Communications: emails, texts, letters between you and landlord or opposing parent
- Court papers: complaint, answer, any prior custody orders, temporary orders, filings
- Income proof: pay stubs, employer statements, tax returns, benefits statements
- Bank statements, expense records, evidence of arrears or payments for children
- Child-related: school records, daycare schedules, medical records, custody/parenting logs
- Witness contact info and brief summary of what each witness can say
- Photos, videos, repair estimates, police reports, protective order paperwork (if applicable)
- Any prior settlement offers or mediation communications

E. Two‑month prep timeline (practical steps)
- Immediately (this week)
  - Give your attorney the documents above; set a prep meeting and ask to run a mock Q&A.
  - Confirm who will appear at the status conference and whether it’s in person or remote.
- Weeks 1–3
  - Complete document collection and create a single organized folder (digital & paper) with labeled exhibits.
  - Identify witnesses and ask them to prepare brief statements; collect contact info.
  - Discuss discovery to request from opposing party (financials, communications, etc.).
- Weeks 4–6
  - Exchange or serve discovery responses; follow up on any outstanding items.
  - Evaluate need for expert (custody evaluator, housing expert) and start engagement if needed.
  - Consider mediation; set a date if recommended.
- Weeks 7–8 (final prep)
  - Meet with counsel for mock Q&A and review likely lines of questioning.
  - Prepare concise timeline/summary fact sheet for attorney and court (one‑page).
  - Confirm court filings and exhibit list to file or present at status conference.

F. Courtroom & deposition response tips
- Be truthful and concise — answer the question asked and avoid volunteering extra information.
- If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing.
- Avoid arguing with the judge or opposing counsel; ask to confer with your attorney if unsure.
- Bring multiple copies of key documents and an organized exhibit binder.
- Dress conservatively, be punctual, and turn off phones.

G. Practical strategic considerations to discuss with your attorney
- Whether eviction could be stayed or whether housing alternatives are needed urgently.
- Whether custody issues should be addressed now or bifurcated from eviction issues.
- Discovery timing — especially financial discovery that could affect both eviction and custody claims.
- Whether temporary orders (custody, financial, stay of eviction) should be sought now.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a concise email or list of questions you can send to Sarah Chen (I can draft it as a message or prepare it as a draft for your review).
- Create a one‑page timeline/fact sheet you can give your attorney or the court.
- Walk through a mock Q&A so you can practice answers.

Which of those would you like me to prepare next?

